Command Shell Execution

(process_name:(cmd.exe OR powershell.exe OR cscript.exe OR wscript.exe OR bash.exe OR sh.exe)
AND parent_name:(w3wp.exe OR httpd.exe OR nginx.exe OR php-cgi.exe OR tomcat*.exe OR java.exe))

-> Detects when web server processes spawn command shells or scripting engines, which is highly unusual in normal operations. It's critical because attackers often use webshells to execute arbitrary commands on compromised servers.
Suspicious Network Connections


(process_name:(w3wp.exe OR httpd.exe OR nginx.exe OR php-cgi.exe OR tomcat*.exe OR java.exe)
AND netconn_count:[1 TO *]
AND -netconn_domain:(*microsoft.com OR *windowsupdate.com OR *adobe.com))

-> Identifies web server processes making unexpected network connections. It's crucial because webshells often establish connections to command and control (C2) servers for data exfiltration or further payload downloads.

Suspicious File Modifications

(process_name:(w3wp.exe OR httpd.exe OR nginx.exe OR php-cgi.exe OR tomcat*.exe OR java.exe)
AND filemod_name:(*\\webroot\\*.php OR *\\wwwroot\\*.asp OR *\\htdocs\\*.jsp))

-> Detects web server processes modifying files in web directories. It's vital because attackers often use webshells to upload, modify, or create malicious scripts in web-accessible directories.



